<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Normans</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Normands, Nortmanni, Normanni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Normans were a people of the medieval Duchy of Normandy who arose from the intermingling of Norse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> settlers with the inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. Their settlement was legitimised when the Viking leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> swore fealty to King Charles III after the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> in 911, and the settlers adopted the language, religion and customs of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. Under their duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> they conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066, while Norman adventurers took southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> and helped found the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under Roger II; Bohemond I founded the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>. In 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> seized mainland Normandy.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Scandinavian raiders first struck the coasts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between 790 and 800. In 820 the first attack sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a>, which became the main route into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>. In the 10th century the war bands settled in more permanent encampments, where they lived with local French women. Settlers began to arrive in the 880s, in colonies around the lower Seine and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula">Cotentin Peninsula</a>. Rollo's contingents included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, Norwegians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norse-gaels">Norse-Gaels</a>, Orkney Vikings and Anglo-Danes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, grew through kinship into the Duchy of Normandy, a great fief of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its dukes ruled autonomously while owing fealty to the French king, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a>, called Richard the Fearless, it became a cohesive principality. Between 1066 and 1204 most kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were also dukes of Normandy, and an Anglo-Norman culture developed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1259">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1259 ended the English claim to Normandy except for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>.</p><p>Normans entered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as warriors in 1017 at the latest. According to William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, Norman pilgrims at Monte Gargano in 1016 were persuaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari">Melus of Bari</a> to return with more warriors against Byzantine rule. The sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tancred-prince-of-galilee">Tancred of Hauteville</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, and Roger the Great Count progressively claimed territory there until the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> was founded in 1130.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The settlers gave up Old Norse for the local Gallo-Romance speech. Their dialect, Old Norman, is the ancestor of the Norman language still spoken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a> and parts of mainland Normandy, and of the Anglo-Norman language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Norman customary law, written down in the 13th century, survives in the legal systems of Jersey and Guernsey. In England the Normans replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> landholding with a feudal system, recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086. Norman builders spread a Romanesque style of rounded arches and massive proportions, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a> was commissioned by Odo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Normans” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the chrysobull of 1082 Alexios I Komnenos granted Venice tax-free trade throughout the empire in return for naval support against the <b>Normans</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Rollo, baptised with the name Robert, extended his territory, and in 924 King Rudolph of West Francia granted the <b>Normans</b> further lands, including the Bessin around Bayeux.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1038 a Byzantine army under George Maniaces, with a corps of <b>Normans</b> and Varangians including Harald Hardrada, crossed the strait of Messina and took Syracuse, but a Muslim counter-offensive won back all the cities lost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They crowned Ordoño IV at Santiago de Compostela in 958, resented the lack of Leonese help when the <b>Normans</b> raided Galicia from 968 to 970, and in 982 crowned Bermudo, who defeated Ramiro III at the battle of Portela de Areas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-galicia">Kingdom of Galicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom inherited from the <b>Normans</b> and Frederick II a centralised royal administration, with justiciars over the provinces, a royal treasury and a high court, but the Angevins granted extensive lands and jurisdiction to French and local barons, and royal authority depended heavily on these lords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The popes were weak military rulers and usually relied on others to protect them: the Franks against the Lombards, the <b>Normans</b> of southern Italy, who became papal vassals at Melfi in 1059, and later France, Spain or Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Military</a></li></ul></section>
